  {
   "id": "lRRaiC-CBgAJ",
   "from": "Juergen Niedballa",
   "author": "0b86ca0cb4b615b0",
   "date": "2018-09-07T10:10:34Z",
   "subject": "Re: Site ID column not showing on recordTable (NA)",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Hi Ali,</div><div>the problem may have to do with the space in the &quot;Site ID&quot; tag name. Can you remove the space and re-run it? You may have to write the metadata of the images to the files again (in digiKam, click the &quot;More&quot; button in bottom right, then &quot;Write metadata to each file&quot;. <br></div><div>If that does not help, the &amp; character in the folder name may cause problems. Can you rename the folder and remove it or replace with an underscore?</div><div><br></div><div>Also, there seems to be an issue with the time zone. You get a warning mentioning time zone &quot;America/Los_Angeles&quot; when you specify &quot;Asia/Shanghai&quot;? That is odd. Can you try running it with timeZone = &quot;UTC&quot; for testing purposes?</div><div><br></div><div>Best,</div><div>Jürgen<br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "sOWbGuLfCQAJ",
   "from": "Ali Y",
   "author": "638816215a1b49a4",
   "date": "2018-09-18T21:29:46Z",
   "subject": "Re: Site ID column not showing on recordTable (NA)",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Jürgen,<div>Thank you for the help! It was the space in the &quot;Site ID.&quot; I don&#39;t seem to have time zone errors when timeZone=&quot;UTC&quot; but I&#39;m not sure how to set the time zone correctly for where my data was collected (Guizhou, China). </div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>Thanks,</div><div>Ali</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "1X-RjtbjCQAJ",
   "from": "David Nicholls",
   "author": "c1310ae8b03a4683",
   "date": "2018-09-18T22:42:14Z",
   "subject": "Re: Site ID column not showing on recordTable (NA)",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Ali<div> </div><div>I am not certain, but for the time zone (if I were the only user), I would use UTC because it is simple and 100% accurate AND with no problems when used with POSIXct in R for any subsequent analysis.  But for field workers checking the time on the cameras at setting it is one extra task to do the conversion to local time.</div><div><br></div><div>My compromise is to set my cameras to local standard time for the entire year. I ask the field workers to not change the time for summer time.  I set the timeZone argument as follows</div><div><br></div><div><pre style=\"color:rgb(255,255,255);font-size:13.333333015441895px;background-color:rgb(50,50,50)\">timeZone               = &quot;Etc/GMT+ANumber&quot;,</pre></div><div><br></div><div>The number require requires care.The documentation explains &quot; <span style=\"color:rgb(255,255,255);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:13.333333015441895px;background-color:rgb(50,50,50)\">Most platforms support time zones of the form </span><span style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13.333333015441895px\"><font color=\"#000000\">Etc/GMT+n</font></span><span style=\"color:rgb(255,255,255);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:13.333333015441895px;background-color:rgb(50,50,50)\"> and </span><span style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13.333333015441895px\"><font color=\"#000000\">Etc/GMT-n</font></span><span style=\"color:rgb(255,255,255);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:13.333333015441895px;background-color:rgb(50,50,50)\"> (possibly also without prefix </span><span style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13.333333015441895px\"><font color=\"#000000\">Etc/</font></span><span style=\"color:rgb(255,255,255);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:13.333333015441895px;background-color:rgb(50,50,50)\">), which assume a fixed offset from UTC (hence no DST). </span><span style=\"color:rgb(255,255,255);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:13.333333015441895px;background-color:rgb(50,50,50)\">Contrary to some expectations (but consistent with names such as </span><span style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13.333333015441895px\"><font color=\"#000000\">PST8PDT</font></span><span style=\"color:rgb(255,255,255);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:13.333333015441895px;background-color:rgb(50,50,50)\">), negative offsets are times ahead of (east of) UTC, positive offsets are times behind (west of) UTC. </span>&quot;</div><div><div><br></div></div><div>I&#39;d welcome advice and correction if I have this procedure wrong.</div><div>David</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  }
 ]
